  "image_name": "f2b0d0b7a0e28fb3d255593cd2988686.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure presents a conceptual path from simple chemistry to “living matter-like properties.” It emphasizes three core features—compartmentalization, metabolic cycles, and replication—and shows that each can exist under equilibrium conditions, but more life-like behavior emerges when they are coupled in out-of-equilibrium systems using thermodynamically activated molecules.\n\nAt the center, overlapping circles indicate that compartmentalization can create localization, phase separation, and concentration gradients; metabolic cycles provide reaction networks and catalysis; and replication enables information transfer, selection, and fidelity. Curved arrows connect these functions, suggesting mutual reinforcement: compartments support metabolic activity, metabolism feeds replication, and replication in turn helps organize the whole system. Together these interactions drive properties associated with living matter, shown by an arrow toward organization, adaptation, cognition, oscillation, and diversity.\n\nThe lower schematic illustrates the chemical basis of this idea. Basic building blocks such as amino acids, lipids, and peptides are activated into higher-energy molecules, which then form a range of assemblies and networks. Some processes are uncatalyzed, while others involve intrinsic catalysis within the assembled structures. A competing deactivation pathway converts activated species into waste, highlighting the need for continual energy input to sustain non-equilibrium, life-like systems.",
